# chktex configuration for this conjecture. Run from this directory:
#
#     chktex -q statement.tex
#
# Four warnings are suppressed, checked hit by hit on 25 August 2026 rather
# than assumed, each with a 100% false-positive rate in this file:
#
#   3   "You should enclose the previous parenthesis with `{}'." Fires on literal
#       set and tuple notation carrying a sub- or superscript --- $\{0,1\}^{n}$,
#       $(sh_{i})_{i \in A}$, $(\bits^{n})^{k}$ --- where the script attaches to
#       the closing delimiter exactly as intended.  (1 hit)
#   8   "Wrong length of dash may have been used." Every hit is an en-dash
#       joining two surnames or two cost measures (Fiat--Shamir,
#       Reed--Muller, Goppa--McEliece, client-space--bandwidth), which is
#       this repository's standing convention; chktex cannot tell a
#       name-joining en-dash from a mis-set hyphen.  (7 hits)
#   13  "Intersentence spacing (`\@') should perhaps be used." Every remaining
#       hit is a `;' or `:' after an uppercase letter, where `\@' has no
#       effect --- TeX's spacefactor logic applies only to `.', `?' and `!'.
#       The `.' and `?' hits were fixed with `\@' rather than suppressed.  (1 hit)
#   38  "You should not use punctuation in front of quotes." Each hit is the final
#       character of a sentence quoted verbatim from the source paper. The
#       punctuation belongs to the quoted text; moving it out would misquote.  (1 hit)
#
# Everything else stays on. As of 25 August 2026 the file is clean under this
# config and `lacheck statement.tex` is silent.

CmdLine
{
    -n3
    -n8
    -n13
    -n38
}
